---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CardGrid from "../../components/CardGrid.astro";
import CardItem from "../../components/CardItem.astro";
import FitFacts from "../../components/FitFacts.astro";
import PageIntro from "../../components/PageIntro.astro";
import SectionBlock from "../../components/SectionBlock.astro";
import {
  boundaries,
  useCases,
  useCasesContent as content,
} from "../../content/use-cases";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={content.pageTitle}
  description={content.pageDescription}
  publishedTime="2026-10-01T00:00:00.000Z"
>
  <PageIntro
    eyebrow={content.eyebrow}
    title={content.title}
    description={content.description}
    anchorLinks={[
      { href: "#short-answer", label: "The short answer" },
      { href: "#test", label: "When it fits" },
      { href: "#contexts", label: "Where it applies" },
      { href: "#boundaries", label: "Where it does less" },
      { href: "#not-covered", label: "What it does not cover" },
      { href: "#required-signals", label: "What a worked example shows" },
      { href: "#tooling", label: "What runs today" },
    ]}
  />

  <SectionBlock id="short-answer" title="The short answer">
    <CardGrid className="grid--three">
      {
        content.shortAnswer.map((item) => (
          <CardItem
            title={item.title}
            description={item.description}
            descriptionTone="default"
          >
            <a slot="footer" href={item.link.href}>
              {item.link.label} &rarr;
            </a>
          </CardItem>
        ))
      }
    </CardGrid>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="test"
    title="When it fits"
    description={content.test.lead}
    variant="alt"
  >
    <div class="stack-sm">
      <ol class="fit-test">
        {content.test.conditions.map((condition) => <li>{condition}</li>)}
      </ol>
      <p class="muted">{content.test.after}</p>
    </div>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="contexts"
    title="Where it applies"
    description="Each names what the system decides, the scored cases that came from it, the standards to adopt in policy or a contract, and the check to run first."
  >
    <div class="fit-contexts">
      {
        useCases.map((context) => (
          <CardItem id={context.id} title={context.title}>
            <div class="fit-context">
              <div class="fit-context__lead">
                <p>{context.decides}</p>
                <p class="muted">{context.why}</p>
                <p class="fit-context__defense small">
                  <span class="fit-context__defense-label">
                    The usual defense:
                  </span>
                  "{context.defense.said}" {context.defense.answer}
                </p>
              </div>
              <FitFacts context={context} />
            </div>
          </CardItem>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p class="small muted">
      "Law in force" names rules that already ask for the same evidence. It is
      not legal advice, and meeting a standard here does not show that a system
      complies with any of them.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="boundaries"
    title="Where it does less"
    description="Kinds of system where one of the conditions fails, and what still applies."
    variant="alt"
  >
    <CardGrid className="grid--three">
      {
        boundaries.map((boundary) => (
          <CardItem
            id={boundary.id}
            title={boundary.title}
            description={boundary.why}
            descriptionTone="default"
          >
            {(boundary.link || boundary.examples.length > 0) && (
              <p slot="footer" class="small">
                {[
                  ...(boundary.link ? [boundary.link] : []),
                  ...boundary.examples,
                ].map((link, index) => (
                  <>
                    {index > 0 && " · "}
                    <a href={link.href}>{link.label}</a>
                  </>
                ))}
              </p>
            )}
          </CardItem>
        ))
      }
    </CardGrid>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="not-covered"
    title="What it does not cover"
    description="It does not judge an institution's goals. Policy, commercial, and political aims are the institution's to choose."
  >
    <div class="stack-sm">
      <p class="fit-not-covered">
        Whatever the goals, the framework asks for the same three things:
        approval that expires unless renewed, a reason with each decision, and a
        named person who can halt the system without penalty.
      </p>
      <p class="small">
        <a href="/standards/core-axioms"
          >The five principles behind every standard &rarr;</a
        >
      </p>
    </div>
  </SectionBlock>

  {
    /* The worked examples had an index of their own, /use-cases/examples,
       which listed the pages these contexts already link. What it had of
       its own was this list. It was a subsection of "Where it applies",
       between the contexts and "Where it does less", the section "When it
       fits" sends the reader to. */
  }
  <SectionBlock
    id="required-signals"
    title="What every worked example must show"
    description="A scenario missing any of these describes a decision. It does not show one that can be enforced or challenged."
    variant="alt"
  >
    <ul class="muted">
      <li>Receipt issued at the moment the decision is enforced.</li>
      <li>
        A named owner who can change the decision, and who it escalates to.
      </li>
      <li>Clocks for acknowledgment, review, and remedy.</li>
      <li>Exception handling with bounded duration and review cadence.</li>
      <li>Remedy path with reversal, compensation, or safe fallback.</li>
      <li>Upheld challenges counted against the rule and sent to its owner.</li>
      <li>
        Generated artifacts (decision log, evidence pack list, appeal record).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small">
      Start with the
      <a href="/use-cases/examples/automated-account-lock"
        >automated account lock</a
      >. It runs every step from receipt to remedy, and the other scenarios vary
      it. The sector comparisons set standard governance beside governance built
      in.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="tooling"
    title="What runs, what is self-report, what is proposed"
    description="A tool that checks evidence and a tool that scores answers are different instruments. So is a standard nobody has adopted yet."
  >
    <CardGrid className="grid--three">
      {
        content.tooling.map((item) => (
          <CardItem
            title={item.title}
            description={item.description}
            descriptionTone="default"
          >
            <p slot="footer" class="small">
              {item.links.map((link, index) => (
                <>
                  {index > 0 && " · "}
                  <a href={link.href}>{link.label}</a>
                </>
              ))}
            </p>
          </CardItem>
        ))
      }
    </CardGrid>
    {
      /* "If your system fits" closed the page as a section of its own: this
         sentence and the two buttons. They end this section, whose second
         card already names the audit. */
    }
    <div class="stack-sm fit-next">
      <p>
        If your system fits, start with one workflow. The delegation audit takes
        it through six questions and lists the permissions nobody on your team
        can justify.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="/diagnostics/delegation-audit">
          Run the delegation audit
        </a>
        <a class="button ghost" href="/#self-test"
          >Take the 60-second self-test</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </SectionBlock>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .fit-contexts {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-block);
  }

  .fit-context {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }

  .fit-context__lead {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    align-content: start;
  }

  .fit-context__lead p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* The defense an institution in this context usually offers, set off from
     the description above it so it reads as the claim the standards answer. */
  .fit-context__defense {
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-3);
    border-inline-start: 2px solid var(--border);
  }

  .fit-context__defense-label {
    font-weight: 650;
  }

  @media (min-width: 860px) {
    .fit-context {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 2fr) minmax(0, 3fr);
      gap: var(--space-6);
    }
  }

  .fit-test {
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.25em;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    max-width: 68ch;
  }

  .fit-not-covered {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 68ch;
  }

  .fit-next {
    margin-top: var(--space-block);
    max-width: 68ch;
  }
</style>
